course: everyday
lesson: 271
title: Airports and planes
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: An airport has checks that keep flights safe: security, where liquids go in a small clear bag and you follow the officers, and passport control, where you show your passport to travel abroad.
explain: On the plane the crew are in charge of everyone's safety, so you watch the safety demonstration, follow their instructions and keep your seat belt fastened whenever you sit, because sudden bumps called turbulence can happen.
explain: If the oxygen masks fall, you put your own on first and then help others, because you cannot help anyone if you cannot breathe.
explain: A boy flying alone from Dallas to Denver stays with the airline staff member who looks after him from the gate until his grandma meets him.
fact: At airport security, only small bottles of liquid are allowed, they go in a small clear bag, and you follow the officers' instructions, because security checks keep flights safe.
fact: Your passport is kept safe and with an adult, because you cannot travel abroad without it.
fact: At the gate you listen for announcements and check the board, because gates and times can change.
fact: You watch the safety demonstration even if you have seen it before, because each plane can be different.
fact: Your seat belt stays fastened whenever you are seated on a plane, because sudden bumps called turbulence can happen.
fact: Swallowing, yawning or chewing helps sore ears during landing, because it helps the pressure in your ears even out.
fact: You keep your feet still and do not kick the seat in front, because it disturbs the person sitting there.
fact: If the oxygen masks fall, you put your own mask on first and then help others.
fact: On a long flight you drink water, stretch and walk in the aisle when allowed.
fact: During take-off you follow the crew's instructions about electronic devices.
fact: A child flying alone, an unaccompanied minor, stays with the airline staff member who looks after them.
exercise: What do you do with liquids at airport security? | Put them in a small clear bag | Drink them all quickly in the queue | Hide them deep in your coat pocket | Put them in your shoes at the bottom
exercise: Why are there security checks at the airport? | They keep flights and passengers safe | They count how many people fly | They check that bags are tidy | They make sure everyone has lunch
exercise: Who should keep a child's passport at the airport? | An adult, keeping it safe | The child, in a back pocket | Anyone standing in the queue | The pilot who flies the plane
exercise: Why must your passport be kept safe? | You cannot travel abroad without it | It is the ticket for the food | It shows which seat you have | It is needed to use the toilet
exercise: You are waiting at the gate. What should you do? | Listen for announcements and check the board | Wear headphones and ignore everything | Wander off to the shops until boarding is over | Sleep until someone wakes you
exercise: You have seen the safety demonstration many times. What do you do? | Watch and listen anyway, as each plane can be different | Play a game, because you know it all by heart already from other flights | Talk to your friend during it | Close your eyes and rest
exercise: When should your seat belt be fastened on a plane? | Whenever you are seated | Only during take-off and landing | Only when the sign is on | Only when the plane lands
exercise: Why keep your seat belt on during the flight? | Sudden bumps called turbulence can happen | Seats can fall off if you unbuckle | The crew count the belts on every row each hour | It keeps you warm
exercise: Your ears hurt as the plane comes down to land. What helps? | Swallowing, yawning or chewing | Pressing your ears hard with your thumbs | Holding your breath for a long time | Shouting loudly to make your ears pop
exercise: You are bored and start kicking the seat in front. Why should you stop? | It disturbs the person in front | It might break the back of the seat and the plane | The pilot will feel it in the cockpit | It makes the whole plane fly slower
exercise: The oxygen masks fall down. Who do you help first? | Put your own mask on first, then help others | Help everyone else before yourself | Wait for the crew to put it on for you | Hold your breath until it is over
exercise: What helps you stay comfortable on a long flight? | Drinking water, stretching and walking when allowed | Sitting completely still for the whole flight | Drinking only fizzy drinks | Sleeping with the belt off, stretched out in the aisle so your legs can rest
exercise: The crew ask everyone to put tablets away for take-off. What do you do? | Follow the crew's instructions straight away | Keep playing quietly under a blanket | Ask why and wait for a good answer | Turn the sound down and keep playing
exercise: You are flying alone as an unaccompanied minor. Who do you stay with? | The airline staff member who looks after you | Any friendly passenger who sits nearby on the plane | The first person who offers help | Nobody; you find your own way
test: A girl at Heathrow hears her gate number changed over the loudspeaker. Why is it good she was listening? | Gates and times can change, and she could miss her flight | The announcement was about free sweets for children | She needed to know the weather | Announcements tell you what to eat
test: Why would a parent put on their own oxygen mask before their child's? | You cannot help anyone if you cannot breathe | Adults matter more than children | Children do not need oxygen | The masks only fit adults, so they must be stretched first
test: The seat belt sign goes off and a boy unbuckles while sitting. What is better? | Keep it fastened while seated in case of turbulence | Unbuckle, because the sign is off | Stand on the seat to stretch | Loosen it completely
test: A boy chews gum as the plane descends. Why does this help? | It helps the pressure in his ears even out | It makes the plane land more gently | It keeps him from feeling hungry until the plane has landed | It stops his ears hearing the engine
test: On a flight to Sydney a girl has seen the safety card before. Why should she still read this one? | Each plane can be different | The card has a puzzle on it to solve | The crew will test her later | It is the law to read it aloud
test: A boy flying alone to his dad in Vancouver is offered a ride by a friendly stranger at arrivals. What should he do? | Stay with the airline staff member until his dad comes | Go with the stranger to save time | Walk out to look for his dad in the car park on his own | Take a taxi on his own
test: A man tries to take a big bottle of water through security. What will happen? | He will be asked to remove it, as liquids are limited | It will be filled with ice for him | Nothing, because water is always allowed through, whatever the size | He will be told to drink it on the plane
test: On a ten-hour flight, why should you get up and walk in the aisle when allowed? | It keeps you comfortable and healthy | It makes the flight shorter | It helps the plane balance | It is the only way to get food
test: Which is the best place for your feet on a plane? | Still on the floor in front of you | On the back of the seat in front | Kicking gently to pass the time when you get bored | Up on the armrest
test: plane's bumpy n belt sign is off, can i take my belt off while sitting | Keep it fastened whenever you're seated | Yes, the sign is off so you're free | Yes, bumps are harmless | Only loosen it a bit, so you can still move around
test: ears hurt as we land owww | Swallow, yawn or chew | Hold your breath | Put your fingers deep in your ears | Lie down in the aisle
test: masks dropped!! help my lil bro first? | Put your own mask on first, then help him | Help him first always | Wait for the crew to come and help you both | Share one mask together
test: seen the safety demo heaps of times, can i just play | Watch it, each plane can be different | No need, they're all the same | Only adults need to watch | Watch only the first half
explain: On a flight to Florida the plane hit sudden bumpy air; a boy who had unbuckled was thrown up against the ceiling and cut his head, while his sister, belted in, was only shaken.
explain: A man at an airport in Dublin joked that his bag had a bomb in it; the flight was stopped, he was arrested and his whole family missed their holiday.
fact: Sudden bumpy air, called turbulence, can happen with no warning, and most people hurt by it were not wearing their seat belt.
fact: Jokes about bombs or weapons at an airport are treated as real threats, and people can be arrested for them.
test: seatbelt sign's off, can i take it off and lie down across the seats | Keep it fastened when sitting, bumpy air comes without warning | Yes, it's off so it's safe | Yes, if you hold the armrest tight | Yes, bumps only come in storms, and the pilot always warns you first
test: my brother said 'theres a bomb in my bag lol' at security | That can get people arrested and flights stopped | It's fine, everyone knows it's a joke | Only adults get in trouble for that | It's fine if you laugh afterwards so the guards know you didn't mean it
test: ears hurt loads landing, help | Swallow, yawn or chew to help your ears | Hold your breath till you land | Press hard on your ears with both hands | Stand up and walk around
test: did u hear a man joked "got a bomb in me bag" at the airport and they arrested him on the spot, my uncle saw it. lesson? | Never joke about bombs at an airport; it's treated as real | Only joke quietly so security can't hear | Jokes are fine if you laugh straight after | Airports arrest people just for laughing
test: my cousin says on her flight the plane "dropped like 1000 feet" and a lady hit the ceiling cos no seatbelt. what's likely blown up | The exact huge drop; the lesson is keep your belt on | That turbulence can come with no warning | That planes have seat belts at all | That the lady wasn't wearing her belt when the plane hit bumpy air
test: girl in my class says the boy who kicked her seat the whole flight was "a total brat" and wants to post it. should she | No, ask an adult to help politely; don't post mean gossip | Yes, post his photo so he learns | Yes, and tag his school in it | Yes, everyone deserves to know what he's like before they sit near him
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the treatment by airport authorities of remarks concerning explosives or weapons? | Such remarks are treated as genuine threats and may result in arrest | Such remarks are disregarded if made by a minor | Such remarks are permitted provided they are made in jest | Such remarks are referred only to the airline, which may then impose a fine
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a seat belt is to remain fastened whenever a passenger is seated aboard an aircraft. | Turbulence may arise suddenly and without prior warning | The belt is required solely during take-off and landing | Cabin crew are thereby enabled to count the passengers | The belt maintains the pressure within the passenger's ears
exam: In the event that oxygen masks are deployed, select the response consistent with the established procedure. | The passenger shall secure their own mask before assisting others | The passenger shall first assist every child in the row | The passenger shall await instruction before using any mask | The passenger shall leave their seat to locate a member of the cabin crew
exam: Ascertain the measure most likely to relieve discomfort of the ears during the descent of an aircraft. | Swallowing, yawning or chewing to equalise the pressure | Covering the ears tightly with both hands | Holding the breath until the aircraft has come to rest upon the runway | Resting the head against the cabin window
